n with minimal dialogue craft but strong language authenticity and grounding. The calculation appears to be cut off mid-computation. Finishing that calculation gives me 4.3 for Father Declan's sample. Now I'm scoring the dialogue from Padraig and Festus, where they deny knowing anyone by that name and mention the bridge no longer has a shop—this grounds them in the setting but the second sentence feels slightly invented, bringing the craft score down to 2, and the overall score comes to 4.3 as well. craft score for the second sample, then moving on to evaluate the third dialogue variant where Padraig gives a slightly longer response with additional context about the person not being known by that name in the area. The second sentence adds grounding through his work at the Letter Office, reinforcing his isolation and character consistency. This variant scores similarly to the previous samples, maintaining the same balance of authenticity and responsiveness while deepening the thematic connection to his role. .25 / 10.25 = 4.3\n\nLooking at the next sample, this response from Cormac is quite thin—just a statement that Tommy O'Brien knows farm ways without any real engagement or follow-up. The character work is minimal, and while the language and grounding are solid, the overall craft suffers from the preamble and lack of depth. Scoring this out with the weighted metrics gives around 3.2.\n\nFor the second sample, Cormac's response is slightly more developed—he acknowledges Tommy's retirement and asks what prompted the question, which adds a bit more character and responsiveness. The mood fidelity improves slightly, though the craft still carries that preamble weakness. This one comes to around 3.7 when I work through the weighted calculation.\n\nThe third sample shows real improvement. Cormac's reference to the stream and drier times feels authentically rooted in his character and concerns, and the dialect marker \"ye\" strengthens the authenticity. The response is pu